# Vendor VM image recipe

The active successful first-boot recipe is **attempt-6/**. This is the KIT2 authorized fallback to the same Snapshot20261121 vendor VM image. Earlier AutoYaST history remains in the parent directory. Choices, failed attempts and documentation are in `../../../parts/microos-kit2.md`.

On the laboratory host:

1. Copy `acquire.sh` to `C1/microos-kit2/` and run it in a durable user unit. It downloads the pinned qcow2 and signed checksum, verifies SHA256 and the official signature, and makes the verified download read-only.
2. Copy `attempt-6/` intact to `C1/microos-kit2/attempt-6/`. Run its `prepare.sh` to make a separate copy, expand its virtual disk to 40 GiB, copy UEFI variables and insert the lab public key. Its exact resulting input is `attempt-6/combustion`.
3. Run `attempt-6/boot.sh` in a durable user unit with MemoryMax=4608M and both unit output streams discarded. It holds one shared flock slot, boots with Combustion, and uses a separate UART to accept only whitelisted numeric command exit records with the fixed random token. The first console is discarded. It tests SSH `exit 37`, privileged `exit 37`, shutdown and emulator exit 0. The producer writes `ready` only on success.
4. Run `attempt-6/register.sh` to move the stopped disk to read-only `C1/base/microos.qcow2`, retain firmware variables at `install/microos/OVMF_VARS.fd`, and write base metadata for SSH, UEFI and q35.
5. Run `attempt-6/validate.sh` in a durable user unit. It creates a disposable clone through the shared runner, requires ordinary exits 0/37 and privileged exit 37, then destroys the clone. Every guest and lifecycle output stream is discarded; only numeric statuses are retained.

The top-level `boot.sh`, `prepare.sh`, `combustion*` and `status-channel.py` preserve superseded attempt-2 inputs; do not use those as the active recipe. Immutable earlier inputs are also in attempt-1 through attempt-5.

The final first-boot script mounts `/home` before creating the user and writing the key, then unmounts it after labeling. This follows the vendor Combustion user-creation example: https://documentation.suse.com/sle-micro/6.2/html/Micro-deployment-selfinstall-images/index.html#combustion-adding-users . The earlier failed supplementary-group assumption was removed based on installer exit 6 and its documented meaning: https://github.com/shadow-maint/shadow/blob/master/man/useradd.8.xml . No group, mount, file or service listing was run on any check machine.

The final preparation supplies the same two NIC definitions, MAC addresses, order and user networks as the shared runner. Standalone setup and a disposable shared-runner clone both passed; the clone was destroyed. Evidence: `../../../raw/microos-kit2/attempt-6-status.log` and `../../../raw/microos-kit2/attempt-6-runner-status.log`. The retained base and firmware template have mode 0444 (`registered-base.txt`).
